00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:39.055
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:40.305 --> 00:00:44.060
A story: the tracking screen that went quiet after 60 seconds.

13
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.665
The grocery app launched live order tracking:

14
00:00:46.945 --> 00:00:50.551
Packed, then Rider assigned, then Out for delivery, then Arriving.

15
00:00:50.831 --> 00:00:52.985
It worked perfectly on office Wi-Fi.

16
00:00:53.265 --> 00:00:56.188
In production, a pattern emerged in support tickets:

17
00:00:56.468 --> 00:00:57.940
The app stopped updating.

18
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.121
I reopened it and my order was already delivered.

19
00:01:01.401 --> 00:01:03.833
The team spent a week on the application code.

20
00:01:04.113 --> 00:01:05.116
It was fine.

21
00:01:05.396 --> 00:01:08.660
The problem was the network path between the phone and the server:

22
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.417
The cloud load balancer had a 60-second idle timeout.

23
00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:21.423
Between Rider assigned and Out for delivery there are often 5 to 10 minutes with no data, so the load balancer silently closed the connection.

24
00:01:22.773 --> 00:01:32.223
The mobile carrier's NAT (carrier-grade NAT on Indian 4G networks) dropped idle TCP mappings even sooner on some networks.

25
00:01:32.503 --> 00:01:35.959
The app's WebSocket client didn't notice the connection was dead.

26
00:01:36.239 --> 00:01:40.037
TCP doesn't tell you a peer vanished unless you send something.

27
00:01:40.317 --> 00:01:48.338
When a rider's phone switched from Wi-Fi to 4G, the client's IP address changed, and every TCP connection it had was gone.

28
00:01:48.618 --> 00:01:59.583
The fix was about 40 lines: a heartbeat every 15 seconds, a reconnect with Last-Event-ID so missed updates are replayed, and aligning timeouts along the whole path.

29
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:01.616
Quote:

30
00:02:01.896 --> 00:02:03.560
The thesis of this chapter.

31
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.384
Every request crosses a chain of networks, proxies and timeouts you don't own.

32
00:02:08.664 --> 00:02:18.264
Good system design treats the network as unreliable, stateful and full of middleboxes, and makes every long-lived interaction survive a dropped connection.

