162
00:08:17.627 --> 00:08:19.248
The cost of a new connection.

163
00:08:19.528 --> 00:08:22.323
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

164
00:08:22.603 --> 00:08:23.541
C to D:

165
00:08:23.821 --> 00:08:25.805
A? api.grocery.in.

166
00:08:26.085 --> 00:08:27.045
D to C:

167
00:08:27.325 --> 00:08:32.787
203.0.113.10 (TTL 60 s).

168
00:08:33.067 --> 00:08:34.005
C to S:

169
00:08:34.285 --> 00:08:34.989
SYN.

170
00:08:35.269 --> 00:08:36.187
S to C:

171
00:08:36.467 --> 00:08:37.256
SYN-ACK.

172
00:08:37.536 --> 00:08:38.475
C to S:

173
00:08:38.755 --> 00:08:39.373
ACK.

174
00:08:39.653 --> 00:08:40.592
C to S:

175
00:08:40.872 --> 00:08:42.749
ClientHello (+ key share).

176
00:08:43.029 --> 00:08:43.947
S to C:

177
00:08:44.227 --> 00:08:45.955
ServerHello, cert, Finished.

178
00:08:46.235 --> 00:08:47.173
C to S:

179
00:08:47.453 --> 00:08:51.421
Finished + HTTP request (GET or orders or 42).

180
00:08:51.701 --> 00:08:52.619
S to C:

181
00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:54.392
HTTP response.

182
00:08:58.242 --> 00:09:06.797
On a 50 ms mobile RTT, a fresh connection costs ~100 to 150 ms before your server does any work.

183
00:09:07.077 --> 00:09:16.186
That's why connection reuse (keep-alive, pooling, HTTP or 2 multiplexing) is one of the highest-leverage performance settings you have.

184
00:09:16.466 --> 00:09:17.959
TCP slow start.

185
00:09:18.239 --> 00:09:26.730
A new connection begins with a small congestion window (~10 segments about 14 KB) and grows as ACKs arrive.

186
00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:32.173
A new connection can't send a 200 KB response at full speed immediately.

187
00:09:32.453 --> 00:09:33.797
Warm connections can.

188
00:09:34.077 --> 00:09:36.295
That's another reason to reuse connections.

189
00:09:37.645 --> 00:09:39.416
Connection teardown and TIMEWAIT.

190
00:09:39.696 --> 00:09:48.997
The side that closes first keeps the socket in TIMEWAIT for 2 times MSL (often ~60 s on Linux) so that late packets aren't misread.

191
00:09:49.277 --> 00:10:02.013
A service that opens and closes thousands of outbound connections per second (no pooling) exhausts ephemeral ports: ~28 K by default on Linux, per destination IP and port.

192
00:10:02.293 --> 00:10:07.008
The symptom is intermittent Cannot assign requested address or connect timeouts.

193
00:10:07.288 --> 00:10:11.021
The fix is connection pooling and keep-alive, not kernel tweaks.

194
00:10:12.371 --> 00:10:15.166
Nagle, delayed ACK and small messages.

195
00:10:15.446 --> 00:10:21.014
Nagle's algorithm batches small writes, and delayed ACK holds acknowledgements briefly.

196
00:10:21.294 --> 00:10:26.670
Together they can add tens of milliseconds to request or response patterns with small writes.

197
00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:37.979
Most HTTP stacks set TCPNODELAY, but check it for custom protocols and for SSE, where you want each small event sent immediately (flush after each event).

198
00:10:39.329 --> 00:10:42.316
Keep-alive, connection pools and head-of-line blocking.

199
00:10:42.596 --> 00:10:49.935
Keep-alive keeps a TCP (+TLS) connection open after a response so the next request can reuse it.

200
00:10:50.215 --> 00:10:54.567
A connection pool is a set of kept-alive connections a client reuses.

201
00:10:55.917 --> 00:10:57.261
Here is a quick table:

