358
00:17:38.485 --> 00:17:39.829
Here is a quick table:

359
00:17:40.109 --> 00:17:42.391
Concept, and What it means for your design.

360
00:17:42.671 --> 00:17:43.525
TTL:

361
00:17:43.805 --> 00:17:46.109
How long resolvers may cache an answer.

362
00:17:46.389 --> 00:17:51.765
Low TTL (30 to 60 s) enables fast failover but means more lookups.

363
00:17:52.045 --> 00:17:55.223
High TTL (hours) is cheap but slow to change.

364
00:17:55.503 --> 00:18:00.581
JVM DNS cache: networkaddress.cache.ttl (security property).

365
00:18:00.861 --> 00:18:05.063
Default is ~30 s when no security manager is installed.

366
00:18:05.343 --> 00:18:07.861
Negative lookups are cached ~10 s.

367
00:18:08.141 --> 00:18:12.066
Never set it to forever in cloud environments where IPs change.

368
00:18:12.346 --> 00:18:14.927
Connection pools ignore DNS changes:

369
00:18:15.207 --> 00:18:18.770
A pooled connection stays on the old IP until it's closed.

370
00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:25.066
Cap connection lifetime (for example, 5 to 10 min) so traffic follows DNS failover.

371
00:18:26.416 --> 00:18:43.739
Kubernetes DNS: service-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local resolves to a ClusterIP. ndots:5 causes several search-path lookups for external names, so use FQDNs with a trailing dot or tune ndots for hot external calls.

372
00:18:44.019 --> 00:18:45.768
DNS as a dependency:

373
00:18:46.048 --> 00:18:48.821
DNS outages look like everything is down.

374
00:18:49.101 --> 00:18:54.264
Cache sensibly, run node-local DNS caches, and alert on resolver errors.

375
00:18:54.544 --> 00:18:57.232
DNS load balancing or GeoDNS:

376
00:18:57.512 --> 00:19:00.541
Returns different IPs by location or health.

377
00:19:00.821 --> 00:19:03.701
Coarse-grained, and clients cache beyond TTLs.

378
00:19:03.981 --> 00:19:08.696
Good for region selection, not for fine load balancing (Ch 08).

379
00:19:10.046 --> 00:19:20.158
Here is the PROPERTIES code. $JAVAHOME or conf or security or java.security (or -Dsun.net.inetaddr.ttl for older setups).

380
00:19:23.008 --> 00:19:25.653
TLS 1.3 and mTLS.

381
00:19:25.933 --> 00:19:36.835
TLS provides confidentiality (encryption), integrity (tamper detection) and server authentication (certificate chain to a trusted CA, and hostname match).

382
00:19:38.185 --> 00:19:39.529
Here is a quick table:

383
00:19:39.809 --> 00:19:41.217
Topic, and Key point.

384
00:19:41.497 --> 00:19:43.481
TLS 1.3 handshake:

385
00:19:43.761 --> 00:19:51.206
1 RTT (versus 2 in TLS 1.2); forward secrecy mandatory; fewer, safer cipher suites.

386
00:19:51.486 --> 00:19:53.939
Session resumption or 0-RTT:

387
00:19:54.219 --> 00:20:00.470
Faster reconnects; 0-RTT early data is replayable (restrict to idempotent requests).

388
00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:01.475
SNI:

389
00:20:01.755 --> 00:20:06.662
Client sends the hostname in the handshake so one IP can serve many certificates.

390
00:20:06.942 --> 00:20:08.158
Certificate chain:

391
00:20:08.438 --> 00:20:10.550
Server sends leaf + intermediates.

392
00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:15.801
A missing intermediate causes errors on some clients only (a classic Android issue).

393
00:20:16.081 --> 00:20:17.467
Certificate expiry:

394
00:20:17.747 --> 00:20:20.115
A top cause of self-inflicted outages.

395
00:20:20.395 --> 00:20:25.942
Automate renewal (ACME, cert-manager) and alert at 30 or 14 or 7 days.

396
00:20:27.292 --> 00:20:28.743
Where TLS terminates:

397
00:20:29.023 --> 00:20:34.356
At the CDN or LB (common), then re-encrypted to the origin; or end-to-end.

398
00:20:34.636 --> 00:20:38.113
Every hop that decrypts is a trust boundary. mTLS:

399
00:20:38.393 --> 00:20:42.511
Both sides present certificates, so the server authenticates the client.

400
00:20:42.791 --> 00:20:49.020
Used service-to-service (service mesh, Ch 17, 23) and for some partner APIs.

401
00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:50.047
Pinning:

402
00:20:50.327 --> 00:20:59.287
Mobile apps pinning certificates must plan rotation (pin the public key or CA, ship backup pins) or a cert rotation bricks the app.

403
00:21:00.637 --> 00:21:01.511
Example:

404
00:21:01.791 --> 00:21:03.242
In the grocery platform.

405
00:21:03.522 --> 00:21:10.626
Customers reach the CDN on TLS 1.3 (HTTP or 2 + HTTP or 3).

406
00:21:10.906 --> 00:21:14.426
The CDN talks to the regional LB over TLS.

407
00:21:14.706 --> 00:21:24.370
Inside the cluster, services use mTLS via the mesh, so payment-service accepts calls only from workloads with the checkout-service identity.

408
00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:33.439
Outbound calls to Razorpay use TLS with the platform's trust store, and go out through a fixed egress IP that Razorpay can allow-list.

