566
00:27:52.855 --> 00:27:54.605
Three AZs for every tier:

567
00:27:54.885 --> 00:27:57.871
Zone-failure tolerance (Ch 02 headroom).

568
00:27:58.151 --> 00:28:01.522
Non-overlapping CIDRs across VPCs or regions:

569
00:28:01.802 --> 00:28:06.751
Future peering and multi-region (Ch 27) won't need renumbering.

570
00:28:07.031 --> 00:28:10.935
Identity-based rules (mTLS) on top of IP rules:

571
00:28:11.215 --> 00:28:14.927
IPs change constantly in Kubernetes; identities don't.

572
00:28:16.277 --> 00:28:18.880
Timeouts across a path: the timeout ladder.

573
00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:20.888
Every hop has its own timeouts.

574
00:28:21.168 --> 00:28:27.973
They must be ordered, or the wrong layer gives up first, which causes retries, duplicate work and confusing errors.

575
00:28:29.323 --> 00:28:30.667
Here is the diagram.

576
00:28:34.517 --> 00:28:35.435
The rules:

577
00:28:35.715 --> 00:28:40.259
Request or response calls: each inner timeout is shorter than the outer one.

578
00:28:40.539 --> 00:28:47.131
A downstream call times out before the service does, the service before the gateway, and the gateway before the client.

579
00:28:47.411 --> 00:28:51.635
Otherwise the outer layer gives up and retries while the inner one is still working.

580
00:28:51.915 --> 00:28:58.677
Long-lived streams: heartbeat interval the smallest idle timeout on the path (NAT, LB, CDN).

581
00:28:58.957 --> 00:29:07.981
Server stream lifetime LB idle timeout, so the server closes cleanly and the client reconnects, rather than a middlebox silently killing it.

582
00:29:09.331 --> 00:29:15.369
Pools: client idle TTL server keep-alive LB idle timeout (§2.4).

583
00:29:15.649 --> 00:29:17.505
Write the ladder in the design doc.

584
00:29:17.785 --> 00:29:20.089
It's an NFR, not a config detail.

