651
00:33:01.358 --> 00:33:07.289
ADR-017 SSE for customer tracking; WebSockets reserved for chat.

652
00:33:08.639 --> 00:33:09.279
1.

653
00:33:09.559 --> 00:33:10.967
Context and Problem.

654
00:33:11.247 --> 00:33:15.769
Tracking updates were delivered over a WebSocket without heartbeats or resume.

655
00:33:16.049 --> 00:33:22.321
Middleboxes silently closed idle connections, and users saw frozen screens (§1.1).

656
00:33:22.601 --> 00:33:23.305
2.

657
00:33:23.585 --> 00:33:24.929
Goals or Non-Goals.

658
00:33:25.209 --> 00:33:37.967
Goals: push status and rider-location updates within 2 s p95; survive disconnects without losing updates; work through CDN, LB and carrier NAT; no sticky sessions.

659
00:33:38.247 --> 00:33:47.420
Non-goals: customer ↔ rider chat (WebSocket, Ch 41); rider app telemetry ingestion (Ch 47).

660
00:33:48.770 --> 00:33:49.517
3.

661
00:33:49.797 --> 00:33:50.757
Requirements.

662
00:33:52.107 --> 00:33:53.451
Here is a quick table:

663
00:33:53.731 --> 00:33:55.203
ID, and Requirement.

664
00:33:55.483 --> 00:33:56.421
FR-1:

665
00:33:56.701 --> 00:34:03.763
Stream order status changes and rider location (every ~4 s while out for delivery) to the order's owner.

666
00:34:04.043 --> 00:34:04.981
FR-2:

667
00:34:05.261 --> 00:34:09.613
A reconnecting client receives all events missed within the last 3 hours.

668
00:34:09.893 --> 00:34:10.939
NFR-1:

669
00:34:11.219 --> 00:34:16.701
Event delivery latency (Kafka publish, then client) p95 at most 2 s.

670
00:34:16.981 --> 00:34:18.027
NFR-2:

671
00:34:18.307 --> 00:34:21.165
Detect a dead connection within 45 s.

672
00:34:21.445 --> 00:34:22.512
NFR-3:

673
00:34:22.792 --> 00:34:26.760
Support 150 K concurrent streams at festival peak.

674
00:34:27.040 --> 00:34:28.128
NFR-4:

675
00:34:28.408 --> 00:34:32.291
Only the order's owner (or support staff with a role) can subscribe.

676
00:34:32.571 --> 00:34:33.616
NFR-5:

677
00:34:33.896 --> 00:34:36.051
Pod deploys must not drop updates.

678
00:34:37.401 --> 00:34:38.105
4.

679
00:34:38.385 --> 00:34:39.665
Capacity estimates.

680
00:34:41.015 --> 00:34:42.359
Here is a quick table:

681
00:34:42.639 --> 00:34:44.943
Item, and Calculation, and Result.

682
00:34:45.223 --> 00:34:47.569
Orders in flight, normal evening peak.

683
00:34:47.849 --> 00:34:53.289
Calculation: ~48 K orders per hour times 35 min lifecycle.

684
00:34:53.569 --> 00:34:55.361
Result: ~28 K.

685
00:34:55.641 --> 00:34:57.711
Orders in flight, festival peak.

686
00:34:57.991 --> 00:34:59.057
Calculation:

687
00:34:59.337 --> 00:35:07.700
Ch 02: first hour about 300 K orders about 83 orders per second sustained times 35 min.

688
00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:10.561
Result: ~175 K.

689
00:35:10.841 --> 00:35:12.057
Concurrent streams.

690
00:35:12.337 --> 00:35:19.121
Calculation: ~85 % of in-flight orders have the tracking screen open at the busiest moment.

691
00:35:19.401 --> 00:35:22.729
Result: ~150 K (sizing target).

692
00:35:23.009 --> 00:35:24.417
Messages to clients.

693
00:35:24.697 --> 00:35:25.764
Calculation:

694
00:35:26.044 --> 00:35:33.575
150 K times (location every 4 s during ~40 % of lifecycle) + status changes.

695
00:35:33.855 --> 00:35:39.337
Result: ~15 K msgs per second + heartbeats 10 K per second.

696
00:35:39.617 --> 00:35:43.137
Memory per stream (servlet async + buffers + emitter).

697
00:35:43.417 --> 00:35:46.233
Calculation: ~30 to 60 KB.

698
00:35:46.513 --> 00:35:47.324
Result:

699
00:35:47.604 --> 00:35:52.596
150 K times 60 KB about 9 GB across the fleet.

700
00:35:53.946 --> 00:35:59.919
Pods ( about 15 K streams per 2 vCPU or 2 GB pod, to be load-tested).

701
00:36:00.199 --> 00:36:01.266
Calculation:

702
00:36:01.546 --> 00:36:07.007
150 K ÷ 15 K times AZ headroom 1.5.

703
00:36:07.287 --> 00:36:08.098
Result:

704
00:36:08.378 --> 00:36:09.466
15 pods.

705
00:36:09.746 --> 00:36:11.410
Redis Pub or Sub channels.

706
00:36:11.690 --> 00:36:12.757
Calculation:

707
00:36:13.037 --> 00:36:14.381
One per watched order.

708
00:36:14.661 --> 00:36:22.170
Result: ~150 K channels, well within Redis capacity; ~25 K publishes per second.

709
00:36:22.450 --> 00:36:23.538
Replay buffer.

710
00:36:23.818 --> 00:36:24.885
Calculation:

711
00:36:25.165 --> 00:36:30.946
150 K streams times ~60 entries times ~150 B.

712
00:36:31.226 --> 00:36:35.642
Result: ~1.4 GB in Redis, TTL 3 h.

713
00:36:36.992 --> 00:36:37.909
Success:

714
00:36:38.189 --> 00:36:39.661
What the estimate tells us.

715
00:36:39.941 --> 00:36:52.357
Connections, not CPU, are the resource to plan: memory per stream, LB and NAT connection limits, and file descriptors (ulimit -n ≥ 65 K per pod).

716
00:36:52.637 --> 00:36:55.304
Location updates dominate message volume.

717
00:36:55.584 --> 00:37:00.896
Throttling them to 1 per 4 s, and only while out for delivery, is the biggest lever.

718
00:37:02.246 --> 00:37:03.014
5.

719
00:37:03.294 --> 00:37:04.489
Proposed design.

720
00:37:04.769 --> 00:37:21.174
SSE over HTTP or 2 (HTTP or 3 at the edge). tracking-router consumes Kafka, appends to a per-order Redis Stream (replay buffer), and publishes to a per-order Pub or Sub channel. tracking-stream pods hold SSE connections,

721
00:37:21.454 --> 00:37:38.606
subscribe to per-order channels only while they have at least one local subscriber, send heartbeats every 15 s, close streams after 100 s with a retry hint (below the 120 s LB idle timeout), and replay from the Redis Stream on Last-Event-ID.

722
