00:38:52.335 --> 00:38:53.850
Alternatives considered.

763
00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:56.544
Here is a quick table:

764
00:38:56.824 --> 00:38:59.363
Option, and Pros, and Cons, and Verdict.

765
00:38:59.643 --> 00:39:01.307
Short polling every 10 s.

766
00:39:01.587 --> 00:39:02.440
Pros:

767
00:39:02.720 --> 00:39:04.107
Trivial, cache-friendly.

768
00:39:04.387 --> 00:39:05.197
Cons:

769
00:39:05.477 --> 00:39:09.083
96 % wasted requests; up to 10 s late.

770
00:39:09.363 --> 00:39:10.173
Verdict:

771
00:39:10.453 --> 00:39:11.584
Fallback only.

772
00:39:11.864 --> 00:39:12.888
WebSocket.

773
00:39:13.168 --> 00:39:14.021
Pros:

774
00:39:14.301 --> 00:39:15.325
Bidirectional.

775
00:39:15.605 --> 00:39:16.416
Cons:

776
00:39:16.696 --> 00:39:20.728
We don't need client, then server; no built-in resume; proxy issues.

777
00:39:21.008 --> 00:39:21.819
Verdict:

778
00:39:22.099 --> 00:39:22.995
Chat only.

779
00:39:23.275 --> 00:39:26.048
SSE + Redis replay + Pub or Sub.

780
00:39:26.328 --> 00:39:27.181
Pros:

781
00:39:27.461 --> 00:39:33.947
HTTP-native, resume built in, HTTP or 2 multiplexing, any pod serves any order.

782
00:39:34.227 --> 00:39:35.037
Cons:

783
00:39:35.317 --> 00:39:38.240
Server, then client only; per-connection memory.

784
00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:39.331
Verdict:

785
00:39:39.611 --> 00:39:40.443
Chosen.

786
00:39:40.723 --> 00:39:42.749
Mobile push notifications only.

787
00:39:43.029 --> 00:39:43.883
Pros:

788
