00:53:34.409 --> 00:53:38.633
SSE for customer tracking; WebSockets for bidirectional use.

979
00:53:38.913 --> 00:53:39.831
Context.

980
00:53:40.111 --> 00:53:41.988
Tracking is server, then client.

981
00:53:42.268 --> 00:53:49.799
The old WebSocket had no heartbeat or resume and failed behind LBs and carrier NAT (§1.1).

982
00:53:50.079 --> 00:53:54.303
We need 150 K concurrent streams with no sticky sessions.

983
00:53:54.583 --> 00:53:55.457
Decision.

984
00:53:55.737 --> 00:54:00.452
(1) Use SSE over HTTP or 2 or HTTP or 3.

985
00:54:00.732 --> 00:54:13.980
(2) Every long-lived connection sends a heartbeat below the smallest idle timeout on its path (15 s) and has a bounded lifetime below the LB idle timeout (100 s ± 10 s).

986
00:54:14.260 --> 00:54:22.900
(3) Events carry monotonically increasing ids, and clients resume with Last-Event-ID from a Redis Stream replay buffer (3 h).

987
00:54:23.180 --> 00:54:29.623
(4) Kafka, then router, then Redis Pub or Sub with per-order channels, so any pod can serve any order.

988
00:54:29.903 --> 00:54:33.359
(5) Pods drain on shutdown with jittered reconnect hints.

989
00:54:34.709 --> 00:54:35.733
Consequences.

990
00:54:36.013 --> 00:54:42.711
(+) Works through standard HTTP infrastructure, and the browser EventSource reconnects automatically.

991
00:54:42.991 --> 00:54:46.021
(+) No stickiness; deploys don't lose updates.

992
00:54:46.301 --> 00:54:55.645
(minus ) Redis becomes a runtime dependency (mitigated: open streams keep heartbeating; push notifications cover status changes).

993
00:54:55.925 --> 00:55:11.199
(minus ) Planned reconnects every ~100 s add ~1.5 K reconnects per second at 150 K streams, which is cheap on warm HTTP or 2 connections but has to be in the capacity plan.

994
00:55:12.549 --> 00:55:30.043
Revisit when: clients need to send frequent messages on the same channel (then WebSocket with the same heartbeat, lifetime and resume rules), or stream counts exceed what Redis Pub or Sub handles comfortably (then shard channels across Redis instances by order id).

