Per-user cap, gauges, counters.

1022
00:56:21.214 --> 00:56:23.518
L4: + per-pod subscriptions.

1023
00:56:24.868 --> 00:56:26.084
Deploy behaviour.

1024
00:56:26.364 --> 00:56:27.196
L1:

1025
00:56:27.476 --> 00:56:28.372
Drops all.

1026
00:56:28.652 --> 00:56:29.463
L2:

1027
00:56:29.743 --> 00:56:30.639
Drops all.

1028
00:56:30.919 --> 00:56:31.772
L3:

1029
00:56:32.052 --> 00:56:33.588
Drain with jittered retry.

1030
00:56:33.868 --> 00:56:36.044
L4: + preStop, readiness.

1031
00:56:36.324 --> 00:56:37.476
Stickiness needed.

1032
00:56:37.756 --> 00:56:39.185
L1: n or a.

1033
00:56:39.465 --> 00:56:40.852
L2: n or a.

1034
00:56:41.132 --> 00:56:43.052
L3: n or a (one pod).

1035
00:56:43.332 --> 00:56:44.207
L4:

1036
00:56:44.487 --> 00:56:45.169
None.

1037
00:56:46.519 --> 00:56:47.394
Question.

1038
00:56:47.674 --> 00:56:49.210
Would you stop at L3?

1039
00:56:49.490 --> 00:56:50.855
Thirty-second answer:

1040
00:56:51.135 --> 00:56:53.589
For a single-instance internal tool, yes.

1041
00:56:53.869 --> 00:56:56.877
For a customer-facing, multi-pod service, no.

1042
00:56:57.157 --> 00:57:04.218
The first deploy with L3 on several pods would send reconnecting clients to pods that have never seen their events.

1043
00:57:04.498 --> 00:57:08.765
The deciding question is simply: can the reconnect land on a different pod?

1044
00:57:09.045 --> 00:57:10.922
In Kubernetes it always can.

1045
00:57:12.272 --> 00:57:13.211
Part 6.

1046
00:57:13.491 --> 00:57:14.771
Production Concerns.

1047
00:57:16.021 --> 00:57:17.173
Failure modes.

1048
00:57:17.453 --> 00:57:18.797
Here is a quick table:

1049
00:57:19.077 --> 00:57:22.959
Failure, and Detection signal, and Blast radius, and Mitigation.

1050
00:57:23.239 --> 00:57:25.437
Idle timeout kills streams silently.

1051
00:57:25.717 --> 00:57:35.253
Detection signal: tracking.stream.closedreason=error spikes at ~60 or 120 s marks; client watchdog reconnects.

1052
00:57:35.533 --> 00:57:36.749
Blast radius:

1053
00:57:37.029 --> 00:57:38.181
Frozen screens.

1054
00:57:38.461 --> 00:57:39.463
Mitigation:

1055
00:57:39.743 --> 00:57:43.775
Heartbeat smallest idle timeout; server lifetime LB idle.

1056
00:57:44.055 --> 00:57:45.207
Proxy buffering.

1057
00:57:45.487 --> 00:57:46.682
Detection signal:

1058
00:57:46.962 --> 00:57:50.269
Events arrive in bursts, or only when the stream closes.

1059
00:57:50.549 --> 00:57:51.765
Blast radius:

1060
00:57:52.045 --> 00:57:53.346
Tracking looks laggy.

1061
00:57:53.626 --> 00:58:00.730
Mitigation: proxy-buffering: off, X-Accel-Buffering: no, Cache-Control: no-transform, flush per event.

1062
00:58:01.010 --> 00:58:04.082
Reconnect storm after deploy or LB restart.

1063
00:58:04.362 --> 00:58:05.557
Detection signal:

1064
00:58:05.837 --> 00:58:08.994
Connection rate spike; 5xx on new streams.

1065
00:58:09.274 --> 00:58:10.490
Blast radius:

1066
00:58:10.770 --> 00:58:12.199
Other pods overloaded.

1067
00:58:12.479 --> 00:58:13.482
Mitigation:

1068
00:58:13.762 --> 00:58:19.842
Jittered retry, drain spread over 1 to 5 s, rolling deploys with maxUnavailable limits.

1069
00:58:21.192 --> 00:58:23.347
Stale pooled connection (outbound).

1070
00:58:23.627 --> 00:58:24.821
Detection signal:

1071
00:58:25.101 --> 00:58:28.173
Intermittent Connection reset after quiet periods.

1072
00:58:28.453 --> 00:58:29.669
Blast radius:

1073
00:58:29.949 --> 00:58:31.293
Random failed calls.

1074
00:58:31.573 --> 00:58:32.576
Mitigation:

1075
00:58:32.856 --> 00:58:37.464
Pool idle TTL server keep-alive; retry idempotent calls once.

1076
00:58:37.744 --> 00:58:40.496
Ephemeral port or NAT port exhaustion.

1077
00:58:40.776 --> 00:58:41.971
Detection signal:

1078
00:58:42.251 --> 00:58:47.819
Cannot assign requested address, NAT gateway errors, connect timeouts to one partner.

1079
00:58:48.099 --> 00:58:49.315
Blast radius:

1080
00:58:49.595 --> 00:58:51.835
All outbound calls to that destination.

1081
00:58:52.115 --> 00:58:53.117
Mitigation:

1082
00:58:53.397 --> 00:58:58.048
Connection pooling, keep-alive, more NAT IPs, fewer new connections.

1083
00:58:58.328 --> 00:59:01.208
DNS resolution failure or slow DNS.

1084
00:59:01.488 --> 00:59:02.683
Detection signal:

1085
00:59:02.963 --> 00:59:06.120
UnknownHostException, latency on first calls.

1086
00:59:06.400 --> 00:59:07.616
Blast radius:

1087
00:59:07.896 --> 00:59:09.411
Anything resolving names.

1088
00:59:09.691 --> 00:59:10.693
Mitigation:

1089
00:59:10.973 --> 00:59:17.224
Node-local DNS cache, sane JVM DNS TTL, FQDNs, resolver alerts.

1090
00:59:18.574 --> 00:59:21.411
DNS change ignored by pooled connections.

1091
00:59:21.691 --> 00:59:22.886
Detection signal:

1092
00:59:23.166 --> 00:59:26.281
Traffic keeps going to the old IP after failover.

1093
00:59:26.561 --> 00:59:27.777
Blast radius:

1094
00:59:28.057 --> 00:59:29.529
Failover doesn't happen.

1095
00:59:29.809 --> 00:59:30.811
Mitigation:

1096
00:59:31.091 --> 00:59:34.483
Max connection lifetime (5 to 10 min) in pools.

1097
00:59:34.763 --> 00:59:37.131
Certificate expiry or broken chain.

1098
00:59:37.411 --> 00:59:38.606
Detection signal:

1099
00:59:38.886 --> 00:59:42.001
TLS handshake errors spike on one client type.

1100
00:59:42.281 --> 00:59:43.497
Blast radius:

1101
00:59:43.777 --> 00:59:45.910
Total outage for affected clients.

1102
00:59:46.190 --> 00:59:47.193
Mitigation:

1103
00:59:47.473 --> 00:59:54.918
Automated renewal, expiry alerts, chain validation in CI. mTLS identity misconfiguration.

1104
00:59:55.198 --> 00:59:56.393
Detection signal:

1105
00:59:56.673 --> 01:00:01.707
403 or CERTIFICATEVERIFYFAILED between services after a mesh change.

1106
01:00:01.987 --> 01:00:03.203
Blast radius:

1107
01:00:03.483 --> 01:00:05.233
Service-to-service calls fail.

1108
01:00:05.513 --> 01:00:06.515
Mitigation:

1109
01:00:06.795 --> 01:00:10.763
Staged policy rollout (permissive, then strict), mesh dashboards.

1110
01:00:11.043 --> 01:00:12.494
Too many streams per pod.

1111
01:00:12.774 --> 01:00:13.969
Detection signal:

1112
01:00:14.249 --> 01:00:17.193
Memory or FD exhaustion, GC pressure.

1113
01:00:17.473 --> 01:00:18.689
Blast radius:

1114
01:00:18.969 --> 01:00:21.017
Pod restarts drop all its streams.

1115
01:00:21.297 --> 01:00:22.299
Mitigation:

1116
01:00:22.579 --> 01:00:28.553
Per-pod cap from load test, readiness false when full, HPA on tracking.streams.active.

1117
01:00:29.903 --> 01:00:31.823
Redis Pub or Sub unavailable.

1118
01:00:32.103 --> 01:00:33.297
Detection signal:

1119
01:00:33.577 --> 01:00:36.159
Router publish errors; delivery lag alert.

1120
01:00:36.439 --> 01:00:37.655
Blast radius:

1121
01:00:37.935 --> 01:00:40.303
No live updates (streams stay open).

1122
01:00:40.583 --> 01:00:41.585
Mitigation:

1123
01:00:41.865 --> 01:00:46.623
Push notifications for status; UI updates delayed banner; Redis HA.

1124
01:00:47.973 --> 01:00:49.061
Observability.

1125
01:00:49.341 --> 01:00:50.685
Here is a quick table:

1126
01:00:50.965 --> 01:00:55.018
Signal, and Type, and Alert or use. tracking.streams.active.

1127
01:00:55.298 --> 01:00:55.959
Type:

1128
01:00:56.239 --> 01:00:57.349
Gauge per pod.

1129
01:00:57.629 --> 01:00:58.717
Alert or use:

1130
01:00:58.997 --> 01:01:04.373
Autoscale on it; alert 80 % of per-pod cap. tracking.stream.closedreason.

1131
01:01:04.653 --> 01:01:05.314
Type:

1132
01:01:05.594 --> 01:01:06.426
Counter.

1133
01:01:06.706 --> 01:01:07.794
Alert or use:

1134
01:01:08.074 --> 01:01:15.242
Rising error share means middlebox kills or network issues. tracking.replays, tracking.replayed.events.

1135
01:01:15.522 --> 01:01:16.183
Type:

1136
01:01:16.463 --> 01:01:17.359
Counters.

1137
01:01:17.639 --> 01:01:18.727
Alert or use:

1138
01:01:19.007 --> 01:01:25.535
High replay volume means frequent disconnects on some network or app version. tracking.delivery.lag.

1139
01:01:25.815 --> 01:01:26.477
Type:

1140
01:01:26.757 --> 01:01:28.506
Timer (event time, then sent).

1141
01:01:28.786 --> 01:01:32.861
Alert or use: p95 2 s pages (NFR-1).

1142
01:01:33.141 --> 01:01:35.466
Connection rate (new streams per second).

1143
01:01:35.746 --> 01:01:36.407
Type:

1144
01:01:36.687 --> 01:01:37.797
LB metric.

1145
01:01:38.077 --> 01:01:39.165
Alert or use:

1146
01:01:39.445 --> 01:01:41.002
Reconnect-storm detection.

1147
01:01:41.282 --> 01:01:44.695
TLS handshake errors by client (UA or app version).

1148
01:01:44.975 --> 01:01:45.637
Type:

1149
01:01:45.917 --> 01:01:47.581
LB or CDN logs.

1150
01:01:47.861 --> 01:01:48.949
Alert or use:

1151
01:01:49.229 --> 01:01:50.786
Chain or pinning problems.

1152
01:01:52.136 --> 01:01:54.248
DNS lookup latency or errors.

1153
01:01:54.528 --> 01:01:55.189
Type:

1154
01:01:55.469 --> 01:01:56.856
CoreDNS metrics.

1155
01:01:57.136 --> 01:01:58.224
Alert or use:

1156
01:01:58.504 --> 01:01:59.528
Resolver health.

1157
01:01:59.808 --> 01:02:02.155
NAT gateway port allocation errors.

1158
01:02:02.435 --> 01:02:03.096
Type:

1159
01:02:03.376 --> 01:02:04.443
Cloud metric.

1160
01:02:04.723 --> 01:02:05.811
Alert or use:

1161
01:02:06.091 --> 01:02:10.741
Outbound exhaustion. http.client.requests + pool stats.

1162
01:02:11.021 --> 01:02:11.683
Type:

1163
01:02:11.963 --> 01:02:12.965
Micrometer.

1164
01:02:13.245 --> 01:02:14.333
Alert or use:

1165
01:02:14.613 --> 01:02:17.088
Outbound latency, pool saturation.

1166
01:02:18.438 --> 01:02:19.377
Security.

1167
01:02:19.657 --> 01:02:25.865
Authenticate every stream (JWT at the gateway) and authorize by order ownership at the pod.

1168
01:02:26.145 --> 01:02:30.582
Return 404 for other users' orders to avoid enumeration.

1169
01:02:30.862 --> 01:02:33.550
Limit streams per user and per IP range.

1170
01:02:33.830 --> 01:02:42.491
Remember that CGNAT puts many real users behind one IP, so per-IP limits must be generous or combined with user identity.

1171
01:02:42.771 --> 01:02:50.473
Location privacy: stream rider location only while out for delivery, only to the order owner, and stop at delivery.

1172
01:02:50.753 --> 01:02:54.635
Never include rider phone numbers in events; use masked calling.

1173
01:02:55.985 --> 01:02:57.223
TLS everywhere:

1174
01:02:57.503 --> 01:03:05.417
TLS 1.2 minimum (prefer 1.3), HSTS on public domains, mTLS inside the mesh.

1175
01:03:05.697 --> 01:03:09.367
0-RTT only for idempotent requests at the edge.

1176
01:03:09.647 --> 01:03:20.207
Egress control: services reach the internet only through NAT with fixed IPs, and outbound destinations are allow-listed (Razorpay, SMS providers).

1177
01:03:20.487 --> 01:03:30.343
Webhooks inbound: verify signatures (Razorpay HMAC), accept only via CDN or WAF, and don't rely on source IP alone.

1178
01:03:31.693 --> 01:03:33.357
Performance and tuning knobs.

1179
01:03:33.637 --> 01:03:34.981
Here is a quick table:

1180
01:03:35.261 --> 01:03:37.095
Knob, and Value, and Guidance.

1181
01:03:37.375 --> 01:03:38.634
SSE heartbeat.

1182
01:03:38.914 --> 01:03:39.682
Value:

1183
01:03:39.962 --> 01:03:41.029
15 s.

1184
01:03:41.309 --> 01:03:48.882
Guidance: min idle timeout on the path; costs ~10 K tiny writes per second at 150 K streams.

1185
01:03:49.162 --> 01:03:50.335
Stream lifetime.

1186
01:03:50.615 --> 01:03:51.383
Value:

1187
01:03:51.663 --> 01:03:53.967
100 s ± 10 s.

1188
01:03:54.247 --> 01:03:55.143
Guidance:

1189
01:03:55.423 --> 01:03:58.282
LB idle timeout; jitter spreads reconnects.

1190
01:03:58.562 --> 01:03:59.778
Client watchdog.

1191
01:04:00.058 --> 01:04:00.826
Value:

1192
01:04:01.106 --> 01:04:03.410
3 times heartbeat (45 s).

1193
01:04:03.690 --> 01:04:04.586
Guidance:

1194
01:04:04.866 --> 01:04:06.167
Detects silent death.

1195
01:04:06.447 --> 01:04:07.706
Reconnect backoff.

1196
01:04:07.986 --> 01:04:08.754
Value:

1197
01:04:09.034 --> 01:04:11.146
1 s, then 30 s, full jitter.

1198
01:04:11.426 --> 01:04:12.322
Guidance:

1199
01:04:12.602 --> 01:04:14.437
Protects servers during incidents.

1200
01:04:14.717 --> 01:04:16.253
Tomcat max-connections.

1201
01:04:16.533 --> 01:04:17.301
Value:

1202
01:04:17.581 --> 01:04:20.055
From load test (for example, 20 K).

1203
01:04:20.335 --> 01:04:21.231
Guidance:

1204
01:04:21.511 --> 01:04:25.223
Default 8,192 caps streams per pod.

1205
01:04:25.503 --> 01:04:26.762
File descriptors.

1206
01:04:27.042 --> 01:04:31.501
Value: ≥ 65,536.

1207
01:04:31.781 --> 01:04:32.677
Guidance:

1208
01:04:32.957 --> 01:04:34.727
One per socket, plus files.

1209
01:04:36.077 --> 01:04:38.019
Outbound pool idle TTL.

1210
01:04:38.299 --> 01:04:39.067
Value:

1211
01:04:39.347 --> 01:04:40.669
30 to 50 s.

1212
01:04:40.949 --> 01:04:44.427
Guidance: server keep-alive (60 to 75 s).

1213
01:04:44.707 --> 01:04:46.733
Outbound max connection lifetime.

1214
01:04:47.013 --> 01:04:47.781
Value:

1215
01:04:48.061 --> 01:04:49.277
5 to 10 min.

1216
01:04:49.557 --> 01:04:50.453
Guidance:

1217
01:04:50.733 --> 01:04:52.333
Follow DNS changes.

1218
01:04:52.613 --> 01:04:54.384
JVM DNS TTL.

1219
01:04:54.664 --> 01:04:55.432
Value:

1220
01:04:55.712 --> 01:04:56.629
30 s.

1221
01:04:56.909 --> 01:04:57.805
Guidance:

1222
01:04:58.085 --> 01:05:00.453
Never cache forever in cloud environments.

1223
01:05:00.733 --> 01:05:03.464
HTTP or 2 max concurrent streams.

1224
01:05:03.744 --> 01:05:04.512
Value:

1225
01:05:04.792 --> 01:05:08.056
Server default (~100 to 250).

1226
01:05:08.336 --> 01:05:09.232
Guidance:

1227
01:05:09.512 --> 01:05:13.160
Raise at the edge if clients multiplex many SSE streams.

1228
01:05:14.510 --> 01:05:15.235
Cost.

1229
01:05:15.515 --> 01:05:16.859
Here is a quick table:

1230
01:05:17.139 --> 01:05:18.377
Driver, and Note.

1231
01:05:18.657 --> 01:05:19.830
Held connections:

1232
01:05:20.110 --> 01:05:27.107
Memory-bound: ~9 GB of stream state across the fleet at 150 K streams (§4).

1233
01:05:27.387 --> 01:05:29.222
LB or CDN pricing:

1234
01:05:29.502 --> 01:05:33.811
Many LBs charge by new connections, active connections and bytes.

1235
01:05:34.091 --> 01:05:40.619
Planned reconnects every 100 s cost ~1.5 K new connections per second at peak.

1236
01:05:40.899 --> 01:05:42.926
NAT gateway data processing:

1237
01:05:43.206 --> 01:05:47.942
Outbound partner calls and image fetches through NAT are billed per GB.

1238
01:05:48.222 --> 01:05:50.611
Use private endpoints for cloud services.

1239
01:05:50.891 --> 01:05:52.193
Cross-AZ traffic:

1240
01:05:52.473 --> 01:05:58.617
Pod ↔ Redis ↔ pod fan-out across AZs is billed per GB.

1241
01:05:58.897 --> 01:06:02.886
Keep payloads small (location events ~150 B).

1242
01:06:03.166 --> 01:06:04.958
Location update frequency:

1243
01:06:05.238 --> 01:06:06.347
The biggest lever:

1244
01:06:06.627 --> 01:06:10.595
4 s versus 2 s halves message volume and battery drain.

1245
01:06:11.945 --> 01:06:12.884
Part 7.

1246
01:06:13.164 --> 01:06:14.871
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1247
01:06:16.121 --> 01:06:16.761
1.

1248
01:06:17.041 --> 01:06:19.409
Long-lived connections with no heartbeat.

1249
01:06:19.689 --> 01:06:20.499
Symptom:

1250
01:06:20.779 --> 01:06:24.427
Streams die silently after 60 s, 5 min of quiet.

1251
01:06:24.707 --> 01:06:25.433
Fix:

1252
01:06:25.713 --> 01:06:30.321
Heartbeat below the smallest idle timeout on the path, plus a client watchdog.

1253
01:06:30.601 --> 01:06:31.305
2.

1254
01:06:31.585 --> 01:06:33.654
Infinite server-side stream lifetime.

1255
01:06:35.004 --> 01:06:47.591
Here is the Java code. before: lives until some middlebox kills it without telling anyone. after: bounded, jittered lifetime; server closes cleanly with a retry hint; client resumes.

1256
01:06:50.441 --> 01:06:51.187
3.

1257
01:06:51.467 --> 01:06:53.643
Events without ids (no resume).

1258
01:06:53.923 --> 01:06:54.734
Symptom:

1259
01:06:55.014 --> 01:06:59.110
Every disconnect loses updates, and users reopen the app to refresh.

1260
01:06:59.390 --> 01:07:00.115
Fix:

1261
01:07:00.395 --> 01:07:05.323
Monotonic event ids and Last-Event-ID replay from a durable buffer.

1262
01:07:05.603 --> 01:07:06.307
4.

1263
01:07:06.587 --> 01:07:09.574
Sticky sessions as the scaling strategy for streams.

1264
01:07:09.854 --> 01:07:10.665
Symptom:

1265
01:07:10.945 --> 01:07:16.854
Uneven load, and deploys or scale-downs drop a whole pod's users with no way to recover.

1266
01:07:17.134 --> 01:07:17.859
Fix:

1267
01:07:18.139 --> 01:07:23.793
Any pod can serve any stream (shared replay + Pub or Sub), so no stickiness is needed.

1268
01:07:24.073 --> 01:07:24.841
5.

1269
01:07:25.121 --> 01:07:27.745
Creating an HTTP client per request.

1270
01:07:29.095 --> 01:07:30.609
Here is the Java code.

1271
01:07:30.889 --> 01:07:46.015
It runs to 17 lines; the full listing is in the chapter. before: new client (and new TCP + TLS handshake, new pool) on every call. after: one client bean with a pooled, kept-alive connector and explicit timeouts.

1272
01:07:48.865 --> 01:07:49.611
6.

1273
01:07:49.891 --> 01:07:51.065
Misordered timeouts.

1274
01:07:51.345 --> 01:07:52.155
Symptom:

1275
01:07:52.435 --> 01:07:56.851
The gateway times out at 5 s while the service keeps working for 30 s.

1276
01:07:57.131 --> 01:07:59.734
The client retries, and the work runs twice.

1277
01:08:00.014 --> 01:08:00.739
Fix:

1278
01:08:01.019 --> 01:08:08.913
The timeout ladder (§2.10): inner timeouts are shorter than outer ones, and it's documented in the design doc.

1279
01:08:09.193 --> 01:08:09.939
7.

1280
01:08:10.219 --> 01:08:13.185
Caching DNS forever or ignoring it in pools.

1281
01:08:13.465 --> 01:08:14.275
Symptom:

1282
01:08:14.555 --> 01:08:19.633
A partner fails over to new IPs, and your service keeps calling the dead ones for hours.

1283
01:08:19.913 --> 01:08:20.638
Fix:

1284
01:08:20.918 --> 01:08:26.742
JVM DNS TTL of ~30 s and a max connection lifetime in pools.

1285
01:08:28.092 --> 01:08:28.775
8.

1286
01:08:29.055 --> 01:08:31.743
IP-based rate limiting for mobile users.

1287
01:08:32.023 --> 01:08:32.833
Symptom:

1288
01:08:33.113 --> 01:08:39.087
A whole city's users behind one carrier NAT IP get 429s together.

1289
01:08:39.367 --> 01:08:40.092
Fix:

1290
01:08:40.372 --> 01:08:48.329
Limit by authenticated identity or device, with IP limits only as a coarse backstop (Ch 18, 39).

1291
01:08:48.609 --> 01:08:49.356
9.

1292
01:08:49.636 --> 01:08:51.193
WebSockets by default.

1293
01:08:51.473 --> 01:08:52.284
Symptom:

1294
01:08:52.564 --> 01:09:00.372
Custom reconnect, resume and auth code, and problems with corporate proxies, for a feature that only pushes server, then client.

1295
01:09:00.652 --> 01:09:01.377
Fix:

1296
01:09:01.657 --> 01:09:06.607
Use SSE for one-way push and keep WebSockets for true bidirectional needs.

1297
01:09:07.957 --> 01:09:08.831
Part 8.

1298
01:09:09.111 --> 01:09:10.455
Interview Q and A.

1299
01:09:11.705 --> 01:09:12.537
Beginner.

1300
01:09:12.817 --> 01:09:13.841
Question 1.

1301
01:09:14.121 --> 01:09:19.647
What happens when you type api.grocery.in or orders or 42 into a browser?

1302
01:09:19.927 --> 01:09:21.292
Thirty-second answer:

1303
01:09:21.572 --> 01:09:25.604
DNS resolves the name to an IP (cached at several levels).

1304
01:09:25.884 --> 01:09:42.247
The browser opens a TCP connection (3-way handshake), negotiates TLS 1.3 (1 RTT, certificate check), sends an HTTP GET over it, and the CDN or LB routes it to a server that builds the response.

1305
01:09:42.527 --> 01:09:45.193
The browser receives, decrypts and renders it.

1306
01:09:45.473 --> 01:09:47.649
The connection is kept alive for reuse.

1307
01:09:48.999 --> 01:09:50.002
Question 2.

1308
01:09:50.282 --> 01:09:53.375
TCP versus UDP: when would you use each?

1309
01:09:53.655 --> 01:09:55.021
Thirty-second answer:

1310
01:09:55.301 --> 01:09:59.738
TCP gives reliable, ordered byte streams with congestion control.

1311
01:10:00.018 --> 01:10:04.626
It's used for HTTP or 1.1 to 2, databases and Kafka.

1312
01:10:04.906 --> 01:10:09.343
UDP gives unordered, unreliable datagrams with no connection.

1313
01:10:09.623 --> 01:10:19.202
It's used for DNS, real-time voice or video, games, and as the base for QUIC (HTTP or 3), which adds reliability per stream on top.

1314
01:10:20.552 --> 01:10:21.597
Question 3.

1315
01:10:21.877 --> 01:10:26.443
What's the difference between polling, long polling, SSE and WebSockets?

1316
01:10:26.723 --> 01:10:28.088
Thirty-second answer:

1317
01:10:28.368 --> 01:10:32.101
Polling asks repeatedly, which is simple but wasteful and late.

1318
01:10:32.381 --> 01:10:35.453
Long polling holds each request until there's data.

1319
01:10:35.733 --> 01:10:42.859
SSE keeps one HTTP response open and the server pushes events, with built-in reconnect and resume.

1320
01:10:43.139 --> 01:10:47.213
WebSockets upgrade to a full-duplex channel for two-way messaging.

1321
01:10:48.563 --> 01:10:49.630
Question 4.

1322
01:10:49.910 --> 01:10:52.875
What does TLS protect, and what doesn't it protect?

1323
01:10:53.155 --> 01:10:54.521
Thirty-second answer:

1324
01:10:54.801 --> 01:11:03.803
TLS provides confidentiality (encryption), integrity (tamper detection) and server authentication (certificate chain and hostname).

1325
01:11:04.083 --> 01:11:11.870
It doesn't protect data at the endpoints: after termination at the CDN or LB, the next hop must be secured separately.

1326
01:11:12.150 --> 01:11:21.985
It doesn't authenticate the client unless you use mTLS, and it doesn't hide the destination hostname completely (SNI is visible without ECH).

1327
01:11:23.335 --> 01:11:24.337
Intermediate.

1328
01:11:24.617 --> 01:11:25.727
Question 5.

1329
01:11:26.007 --> 01:11:31.724
Compare HTTP or 1.1, HTTP or 2 and HTTP or 3.

1330
01:11:32.004 --> 01:11:33.241
Which would you use where?

1331
01:11:33.521 --> 01:11:34.887
Thirty-second answer:

1332
01:11:35.167 --> 01:11:40.159
HTTP or 1.1 handles one request at a time per connection.

1333
01:11:40.439 --> 01:11:50.188
HTTP or 2 multiplexes many streams over one TCP connection with header compression, but TCP-level HOL blocking remains.

1334
01:11:50.468 --> 01:11:57.956
HTTP or 3 runs over QUIC (UDP) with independent streams, faster handshakes and connection migration.

1335
01:11:58.236 --> 01:12:11.356
Use HTTP or 2 and 3 at the edge (mobile benefits from HTTP or 3), and HTTP or 2 (gRPC) or keep-alive HTTP or 1.1 internally.

1336
01:12:12.706 --> 01:12:13.815
Question 6.

1337
01:12:14.095 --> 01:12:18.042
Why does connection reuse matter so much, and how do you configure it?

1338
01:12:18.322 --> 01:12:19.687
Thirty-second answer:

1339
01:12:19.967 --> 01:12:34.559
A new connection costs DNS + TCP (1 RTT) + TLS (1 RTT) plus slow start, which is 100 plus ms on mobile and meaningful even inside a data centre at high QPS.

1340
01:12:34.839 --> 01:12:45.314
Reuse via keep-alive and pools, set pool size from Little's Law, and order idle timeouts: client idle TTL server keep-alive LB idle timeout.

1341
01:12:46.664 --> 01:12:47.752
Question 7.

1342
01:12:48.032 --> 01:12:52.171
How does DNS work, and what DNS problems have you seen in production?

1343
01:12:52.451 --> 01:12:53.816
Thirty-second answer:

1344
01:12:54.096 --> 01:13:00.859
Recursive resolvers walk root, then TLD, then authoritative servers and cache answers for their TTL.

1345
01:13:01.139 --> 01:13:02.547
Common production issues:

1346
01:13:02.827 --> 01:13:17.504
JVMs or pools holding stale IPs after failover, ndots in Kubernetes multiplying lookups, resolver overload or outages making everything look down, and negative caching hiding a record that was just created.

1347
01:13:18.854 --> 01:13:19.878
Question 8.

1348
01:13:20.158 --> 01:13:22.846
When would you choose SSE over WebSockets?

1349
01:13:23.126 --> 01:13:24.726
Design live order tracking.

1350
01:13:25.006 --> 01:13:26.371
Thirty-second answer:

1351
01:13:26.651 --> 01:13:36.422
Tracking is server, then client, so use SSE: plain HTTP, HTTP or 2 multiplexing, built-in reconnect with Last-Event-ID.

1352
01:13:36.702 --> 01:13:37.662
The design:

1353
01:13:37.942 --> 01:13:45.238
Kafka events go to a router that writes a per-order Redis Stream (replay) and publishes to a per-order Pub or Sub channel.

1354
01:13:45.518 --> 01:13:55.651
Stream pods subscribe only for orders they serve, send heartbeats every 15 s, close every ~100 s with a retry hint, and replay on reconnect.

1355
01:13:57.001 --> 01:13:58.068
Question 9.

1356
01:13:58.348 --> 01:14:04.001
What are L4 and L7 load balancers, and why does it matter for long-lived connections?

1357
01:14:04.281 --> 01:14:05.647
Thirty-second answer:

1358
01:14:05.927 --> 01:14:11.836
L4 balances TCP or UDP connections without understanding HTTP.

1359
01:14:12.116 --> 01:14:18.047
L7 terminates HTTP, routes by path or headers, and can retry and inspect.

1360
01:14:18.327 --> 01:14:26.177
For long-lived connections, L7 LBs impose HTTP idle and response timeouts and may buffer responses.

1361
01:14:26.457 --> 01:14:33.625
L4 LBs balance connections, not requests, so long-lived streams can leave load uneven after scaling.

1362
01:14:34.975 --> 01:14:35.743
Senior.

1363
01:14:36.023 --> 01:14:37.069
Question 10.

1364
01:14:37.349 --> 01:14:40.485
Users say live tracking 'freezes' after a minute or two.

1365
01:14:40.765 --> 01:14:42.045
How do you debug it?

1366
01:14:42.325 --> 01:14:43.690
Thirty-second answer:

1367
01:14:43.970 --> 01:14:46.039
Suspect idle timeouts on the path:

1368
01:14:46.319 --> 01:14:50.522
LB (often 60 s), carrier NAT, proxies, and buffering.

1369
01:14:50.802 --> 01:15:03.986
Check close-reason metrics and timing (do disconnects cluster at 60 s?), LB and ingress timeouts, whether heartbeats exist and are flushed, whether buffering is on, and whether clients have a watchdog and resume.

1370
01:15:05.336 --> 01:15:06.467
Question 11.

1371
01:15:06.747 --> 01:15:13.595
Outbound calls to a payment partner intermittently fail with connection resets or 'cannot assign requested address'.

1372
01:15:13.875 --> 01:15:14.877
What's happening?

1373
01:15:15.157 --> 01:15:16.523
Thirty-second answer:

1374
01:15:16.803 --> 01:15:22.456
Resets usually mean stale pooled connections (the partner or LB closed idle ones first).

1375
01:15:22.736 --> 01:15:29.776
Cannot assign requested address means ephemeral or NAT port exhaustion from opening too many new connections.

1376
01:15:30.056 --> 01:15:40.872
The fixes are proper pooling and keep-alive, idle TTL below the partner's timeout, a retry on reset for idempotent calls, and more NAT IPs if needed.

1377
01:15:42.222 --> 01:15:43.310
Question 12.

1378
01:15:43.590 --> 01:15:47.878
How do you handle a reconnect storm after a deploy or an LB failover?

1379
01:15:48.158 --> 01:15:49.523
Thirty-second answer:

1380
01:15:49.803 --> 01:16:11.457
Prevent synchronization (jittered lifetimes, jittered retry hints on drain, rolling deploys with limited unavailability), protect the servers (connection admission limits, readiness false when full, gateway rate limiting of new streams), and make reconnects cheap (HTTP or 2, TLS resumption, replay from Redis rather than recomputing state).

1381
01:16:12.807 --> 01:16:14.087
Question 13.

1382
01:16:14.367 --> 01:16:16.692
Explain timeouts across a request path.

1383
01:16:16.972 --> 01:16:18.145
How do you set them?

1384
01:16:18.425 --> 01:16:19.791
Thirty-second answer:

1385
01:16:20.071 --> 01:16:25.831
Every hop has timeouts: client, CDN, LB, gateway, service, downstream, DB.

1386
01:16:26.111 --> 01:16:32.617
For request or response, inner timeouts must be shorter than outer ones, and derived from a latency budget.

1387
01:16:32.897 --> 01:16:38.145
For streams, heartbeat min idle timeout, and server lifetime LB idle timeout.

1388
01:16:38.425 --> 01:16:41.561
Pools: client idle server keep-alive LB idle.

1389
01:16:42.911 --> 01:16:44.106
Question 14.

1390
01:16:44.386 --> 01:16:49.186
A partner rotated their TLS certificate and some of your services started failing.

1391
01:16:49.466 --> 01:16:50.511
Why only some?

1392
01:16:50.791 --> 01:16:52.157
Thirty-second answer:

1393
01:16:52.437 --> 01:17:10.399
Likely causes: different trust stores (an old base image missing the new CA or intermediate), certificate pinning in some clients, a partner chain missing an intermediate that only some clients can fetch or cache, or different TLS versions and ciphers across JVM versions.

1394
01:17:11.749 --> 01:17:12.645
Architect.

1395
01:17:12.925 --> 01:17:14.120
Question 15.

1396
01:17:14.400 --> 01:17:18.731
Design the network topology for the grocery platform in one region.

1397
01:17:19.011 --> 01:17:20.376
Thirty-second answer:

1398
01:17:20.656 --> 01:17:26.629
CDN + WAF at the edge (TLS 1.3, HTTP or 2 and 3).

1399
01:17:26.909 --> 01:17:39.069
A VPC with public subnets only for LBs and NAT, private app subnets across 3 AZs for Kubernetes, and private data subnets for PostgreSQL, Redis and Kafka.

1400
01:17:39.349 --> 01:17:54.432
Egress via NAT with fixed IPs for partners, private endpoints for cloud services, mTLS in the mesh with identity-based policies, NetworkPolicies as defense in depth, and non-overlapping CIDRs for future regions.

1401
01:17:55.782 --> 01:17:56.977
Question 16.

1402
01:17:57.257 --> 01:18:03.699
How would you set platform-wide standards for timeouts, retries and connections across 26 services?

1403
01:18:03.979 --> 01:18:05.345
Thirty-second answer:

1404
01:18:05.625 --> 01:18:28.110
Publish a standard: every client has explicit connect or read timeouts derived from per-route latency budgets, pools sized by Little's Law with idle TTL and max lifetime rules, retries only for idempotent operations with budgets and jitter, deadline propagation, and heartbeat or lifetime or resume rules for long-lived connections.

1405
01:18:28.390 --> 01:18:33.382
Enforce it through shared client libraries or a mesh, config linting, and dashboards.

1406
01:18:34.732 --> 01:18:36.076
Question 17.

1407
01:18:36.356 --> 01:18:38.980
Should the grocery platform adopt a service mesh?

1408
01:18:39.260 --> 01:18:40.625
What does it give and cost?

1409
01:18:40.905 --> 01:18:42.271
Thirty-second answer:

1410
01:18:42.551 --> 01:18:54.753
A mesh gives mTLS with workload identity, identity-based authorization, uniform retries, timeouts and telemetry, and traffic shifting for canaries, without changing app code.

1411
01:18:55.033 --> 01:19:06.063
It costs resources (sidecar CPU and memory, or ambient-mode components), latency (sub-millisecond per hop), and operational complexity (upgrades, debugging).

1412
01:19:06.343 --> 01:19:15.708
Adopt it when the service count and security requirements justify it, as they do at 26 services with PCI and DPDP obligations.

1413
01:19:17.058 --> 01:19:18.231
Question 18.

1414
01:19:18.511 --> 01:19:22.181
Your platform must serve customers across India from one region.

1415
01:19:22.461 --> 01:19:25.639
How do you minimize latency for users far from Mumbai?

1416
01:19:25.919 --> 01:19:27.285
Thirty-second answer:

1417
01:19:27.565 --> 01:19:48.557
Terminate TLS at a CDN with Indian edge locations (saves handshake RTTs), cache catalog and images at the edge, enable HTTP or 3 for mobile, keep connections warm from edge to origin, reduce round trips (fewer sequential calls, aggregated BFF responses), and compress payloads.

1418
01:19:48.837 --> 01:19:55.301
Multi-region only when latency or availability requirements justify it (Ch 27).

1419
01:19:56.651 --> 01:19:57.888
Question 19.

1420
01:19:58.168 --> 01:20:01.261
How do you design for zero trust inside the platform?

1421
01:20:01.541 --> 01:20:02.907
Thirty-second answer:

1422
01:20:03.187 --> 01:20:04.915
Don't trust network location.

1423
01:20:05.195 --> 01:20:17.909
Every call is authenticated (mTLS workload identity, plus end-user tokens where relevant), authorized by policy (service A may call B's specific routes), encrypted, and logged.

1424
01:20:18.189 --> 01:20:25.187
Least privilege is enforced for services, humans (just-in-time access) and data (per-service credentials).

1425
01:20:25.467 --> 01:20:28.709
Network segmentation remains as defense in depth.

1426
01:20:30.059 --> 01:20:31.169
Question 20.

1427
01:20:31.449 --> 01:20:36.462
What would make you choose HTTP or 3 for the rider app, and how would you roll it out?

1428
01:20:36.742 --> 01:20:38.107
Thirty-second answer:

1429
01:20:38.387 --> 01:20:46.259
Riders move constantly between networks (Wi-Fi ↔ 4G, cell handovers), and their connections are lossy.

1430
01:20:46.539 --> 01:20:53.537
QUIC's connection migration, 0 or 1-RTT setup and independent streams reduce stalls and reconnects.

1431
01:20:53.817 --> 01:21:08.665
Roll it out via the CDN or LB with HTTP or 3 enabled, clients that fall back to HTTP or 2 when UDP is blocked, and metrics by transport comparing reconnect rate, latency and battery use.

1432
01:21:10.015 --> 01:21:10.911
Part 9.

1433
01:21:11.191 --> 01:21:12.599
Summary and Practice.

1434
01:21:13.849 --> 01:21:14.937
Key takeaways.

1435
01:21:15.217 --> 01:21:23.515
A request crosses DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP and many middleboxes, each with its own caching and timeouts.

1436
01:21:23.795 --> 01:21:28.382
New connections are expensive (DNS + 2 RTT + slow start).

1437
01:21:28.662 --> 01:21:32.161
Reuse them with keep-alive and pools sized by Little's Law.

1438
01:21:32.441 --> 01:21:36.878
Order idle timeouts: client pool idle server keep-alive LB idle.

1439
01:21:37.158 --> 01:21:40.806
Cap connection lifetime so DNS changes take effect.

1440
01:21:41.086 --> 01:21:46.611
HTTP or 2 multiplexes over TCP (TCP HOL remains).

1441
01:21:46.891 --> 01:21:54.977
HTTP or 3 or QUIC removes cross-stream HOL and survives network changes, which matters most on mobile.

1442
01:21:56.327 --> 01:22:03.260
DNS is a dependency: sane JVM TTLs, node-local caches, FQDNs, and monitoring.

1443
01:22:03.540 --> 01:22:06.356
TLS 1.3 is 1 RTT.

1444
01:22:06.636 --> 01:22:11.223
0-RTT is replayable, so use it for idempotent requests only.

1445
01:22:11.503 --> 01:22:15.364
Automate certificates, and use mTLS for service identity.

1446
01:22:15.644 --> 01:22:20.956
Use SSE for server, then client push, and WebSockets for bidirectional messaging.

1447
01:22:21.236 --> 01:22:27.017
Every long-lived connection needs a heartbeat, a bounded lifetime, and resume by event id.

1448
01:22:27.297 --> 01:22:31.863
Carrier NAT and cloud NAT have idle timeouts and port limits.

1449
01:22:32.143 --> 01:22:35.812
Heartbeat, pool, and don't rate-limit mobile users by IP.

1450
01:22:37.162 --> 01:22:46.207
Topology: public subnets only for LBs and NAT, private app and data tiers, fixed egress IPs, identity-based rules.

1451
01:22:46.487 --> 01:22:50.050
Write the timeout ladder into the design doc as an NFR.

1452
01:22:51.400 --> 01:22:52.723
Decision cheat sheet.

1453
01:22:53.003 --> 01:22:54.347
Here is a quick table:

1454
01:22:54.627 --> 01:22:56.077
Situation, and Choose.

1455
01:22:56.357 --> 01:22:59.984
Server, then client updates (tracking, notifications feed):

1456
01:23:00.264 --> 01:23:02.952
SSE + heartbeat + Last-Event-ID.

1457
01:23:03.232 --> 01:23:05.195
Bidirectional real-time (chat):

1458
01:23:05.475 --> 01:23:07.715
WebSocket + heartbeat + resume.

1459
01:23:07.995 --> 01:23:09.701
Very low-frequency updates:

1460
01:23:09.981 --> 01:23:12.008
Polling (or push notifications).

1461
01:23:12.288 --> 01:23:14.315
Mobile clients on lossy networks:

1462
01:23:14.595 --> 01:23:16.643
HTTP or 3 at the edge.

1463
01:23:16.923 --> 01:23:18.587
Service-to-service RPC:

1464
01:23:18.867 --> 01:23:25.352
HTTP or 2 (gRPC) or keep-alive HTTP or 1.1 + pools.

1465
01:23:25.632 --> 01:23:27.189
Streams across many pods:

1466
01:23:27.469 --> 01:23:30.648
Shared replay buffer + Pub or Sub, no stickiness.

1467
01:23:30.928 --> 01:23:33.360
Partner API with IP allow-listing:

1468
01:23:33.640 --> 01:23:35.667
NAT with fixed egress IPs.

1469
01:23:35.947 --> 01:23:37.717
Fast failover via DNS:

1470
01:23:37.997 --> 01:23:43.587
Low TTL + JVM DNS TTL 30 s + pool max lifetime.

1471
01:23:44.937 --> 01:23:46.067
Exercises.

1472
01:23:46.347 --> 01:23:47.691
Here is a quick table:

1473
01:23:47.971 --> 01:23:49.827
Band, and Exercise, and Hint.

1474
01:23:50.107 --> 01:23:50.939
B1.

1475
01:23:51.219 --> 01:23:52.286
